<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sergei Prokofiev</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1891">1891</a>–<a href="/years/1953">1953</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sergei Prokofiev was a Russian composer and pianist who left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> after the revolution and returned to settle in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1936. His works include the ballet Romeo and Juliet, the children's tale Peter and the Wolf, the music for Eisenstein's film <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-nevsky">Alexander Nevsky</a>, seven symphonies and the opera War and Peace. Condemned for formalism in 1948, he died on the same day as Stalin.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sergei-prokofiev.jpg" alt="Sergei Prokofiev" width="320" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bain News Service, publisher · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sergei_Prokofiev_circa_1918_over_Chair_Bain.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sergei Sergeyevich Prokofiev was born on 27 April 1891 (15 April in the old style) on the rural estate of Sontsovka, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. His father was an agronomist and his mother came from a family of former serfs. In 1902 she met Sergei Taneyev, the director of the Moscow Conservatory, who arranged for Reinhold Glière to teach the boy at Sontsovka that summer. At eleven Prokofiev tried to write a symphony. Later he complained that Glière had given him conventional phrase structure that he had to unlearn, and he began experimenting with dissonant harmony in short piano pieces.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Prokofiev studied at the Saint Petersburg Conservatory and in 1914 won its competition for the best piano students by playing his own Piano Concerto No. 1. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> he met the impresario Sergei Diaghilev, who rejected his first ballet as non-Russian and commissioned Chout instead. In the summer of 1917 he wrote the Classical Symphony, in the style he said <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-haydn">Joseph Haydn</a> would have used had he lived then. After the Revolution he left with the approval of the commissar Anatoly Lunacharsky and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> in August 1918. His opera The Love for Three Oranges was delayed by the illness and death of the Chicago Opera's director, and in 1920 he left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><p>He toured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1927 and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> with his family in 1936, after four years of moving between Moscow and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. That year he wrote Peter and the Wolf for Natalya Sats's Central Children's Theatre. His Cantata for the 20th Anniversary of the October Revolution was effectively blocked and was not performed until 1966. The German invasion of 22 June 1941 led him to begin War and Peace, for which Mira Mendelson, who became his second wife, co-wrote the libretto. He was evacuated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a>, where he wrote the first version.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 10 February 1948, the day before the decree of the Zhdanov Doctrine was published, Prokofiev was honoured at the Kremlin as a People's Artist of the RSFSR. He was then attacked for anti-democratic formalism, but he kept the support of younger performers, among them Sviatoslav Richter, for whom he wrote his Ninth Piano Sonata. He died on 5 March 1953 at the age of 61, the same day as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a>. Crowds mourning Stalin made it impossible to hold the funeral service at the Composers' Union headquarters, and his coffin was carried by hand through back streets. About thirty people attended, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitri-shostakovich">Dmitri Shostakovich</a> among them. He is buried in the Novodevichy Cemetery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sergei Prokofiev” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Soviet culture combined official socialist realism with world-class achievement in music, ballet, film and literature, from <b>Sergei Prokofiev</b>, Dmitri Shostakovich and Sergei Eisenstein to Boris Pasternak and Aleksandr Solzhenitsyn, whose account of the Gulag shaped Western views of the system.</blockquote><a href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Sontsivka, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.05,37.19&amp;year=1931" aria-label="Sontsivka on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Sergei Prokofiev"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5410 5992h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5410 5992h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.05,37.19&amp;year=1931">What was here in 1931? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
